# R23 Letters Home (side quest, delivery): Geneva's last letters, one of them Walder Pye's. By hand, polished by Gemini, unvoiced.

~ start
geneva_fairchild [weary]: The mail won't go out tomorrow, nor for a good while after. These are the last letters, and every one has a soul waiting in the Round to read it. Would you carry them? I must mind the cart.
give wv_last_letters
set wv_letters_asked
quest letters_home asked
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask who the letters are meant for -> who
* [if flag.letters_n >= 3] Tell her every letter has been read -> done
* [ends:letters_home] Say there is no time to deliver them -> unsent
* Take the satchel and get moving -> END

~ who
geneva_fairchild [neutral]: Sergeant Gaunt has word from his mother, Mr Quill from a publisher, and Miss Ledger from the county seat. The fourth goes to Mrs Grey from Walder Pye, a traveller who died. She ought to have his own words.
-> menu

~ progress
geneva_fairchild [neutral]: Have you found them all yet? I am keeping tally on the slate.
-> menu

~ after
if quest.letters_home == delivered
geneva_fairchild [smile]: The satchel is empty. I scarce thought I'd see the bottom of it tonight. Thank you.
else
geneva_fairchild [sad]: They are pinned to the board now, with the wind turning the pages. I haven't the heart to look.
endif
-> END

~ done
geneva_fairchild [smile]: Every last one in the right hands. Thank you. I'll mark the ledger clear.
take wv_last_letters
quest letters_home delivered
set letters_done
inspiration party
-> END

~ unsent
geneva_fairchild [sad]: Then I'll tack them to the notice board and let the wind read them.
take wv_last_letters
quest letters_home unsent
set letters_done
-> END

~ cart
Narrator: You find a post cart half-empty of mail and half-full of storm lanterns, smelling of wet canvas and sealing wax. Geneva keeps a ledger on her knee, ticking off each name as a letter leaves her hands.
-> END

~ to_gaunt
Narrator: Sergeant Gaunt cracks the wax, reads a single line, and goes the colour of cold suet.
sgt_gaunt [afraid]: My mother tells me to come home. Calls me a coward, says she's always known it, and says she is proud of me regardless. The woman sees right through me, and I wish it entered in the record that she is entirely right.
set letter_gaunt
set letters_n += 1
-> END

~ to_quill
Narrator: Ambrose Quill reads the page twice over, shaping the syllables with his lips.
ambrose_quill [sad]: A rejection from the city, composed entirely in rhyming couplets. It is, by some distance, the finest verse that has ever refused me. I shall have it framed.
set letter_quill
set letters_n += 1
-> END

~ to_tamsin
Narrator: Tamsin Ledger slits the county envelope with a bone knife and reads it on her feet.
tamsin_ledger [neutral]: The audit office has cancelled my leave and ordered me back by month's end. They appear unaware that the month is not ending. I shall send a memorandum.
set letter_tamsin
set letters_n += 1
-> END

~ to_marianna
Narrator: Marianna Grey holds Walder Pye's letter in both hands, her thumbs tracing the folded paper before she breaks the seal.
marianna_grey [sad]: He asks me to tell the mare he is sorry, and that he meant to come back for her. I'll go tell her now. She's waiting in the stall, and she'll know what he means.
set letter_marianna
set letters_n += 1
-> END
